M¸␀ûÖ GitHubRateLimit is how the fake refuses the requests one installation makes
 beyond its allowance, the way GitHub refuses them: a 403 or 429 whose
 remaining is zero for REST and a 200 carrying a RATE_LIMITED error for
 GraphQL, alongside the headers GitHub reports the allowance in on every
 response. GitHub meters a GitHub App per installation, so every organization
 the App is installed on spends its own copy of this allowance.

 https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api


M¸
â
M␀ˇ{ Whether the fake counts requests against an allowance. A fake that doesn't
 answers every request and reports no headers.


M␀ˇ

M␀ˇ

M␀ˇ
í
MÉ#É How many requests the core allowance holds. GitHub allows an installation
 5,000 an hour, and a larger organization up to 12,500.


MÉ

MÉ

MÉ!"
Í
Mà$€ How many points the GraphQL allowance holds. GitHub prices a query by the
 connections it walks; every query the client issues costs one point, so
 this is a query count. Zero holds what GitHub allows an installation.


Mà

Mà

Mà"#
‹
Mç&Õ How long an allowance lasts, which is what the reset the fake reports is
 measured to. Zero lasts GitHub's own hour; a shorter window is how a reset
 near enough for a client to wait through is produced.


Mç

Mç!

Mç$%
–
Mî&¡ How many requests an installation may make within a minute before one is
 refused with a secondary rate limit. Zero allows what GitHub allows: 900
 points a minute for the REST API, where a read costs a point.

 https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api#about-secondary-rate-limits


Mî

Mî!

Mî$%
h
